window.__labLoaded({"id":"5.3","title":"Confidence Intervals for a Proportion","goal":"Build 95% confidence intervals for a proportion with the Wald and Wilson methods, and see why Wilson is safer for small samples.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"A healthy poll: 476 of 850","text":"The lesson's poll. statsmodels' proportion_confint takes the count of successes, the sample size, and a method: 'normal' is the classic Wald interval, 'wilson' is the Wilson score interval.","code":"from statsmodels.stats.proportion import proportion_confint\n\nx, n = 476, 850\nprint(f'p-hat = {x / n:.3f}')\nfor method in ('normal', 'wilson'):\n    low, high = proportion_confint(x, n, alpha=0.05, method=method)\n    print(f'{method:7s}: ({low:.4f}, {high:.4f})')","out":"p-hat = 0.560\nnormal : (0.5266, 0.5934)\nwilson : (0.5264, 0.5930)","figs":[]},{"title":"A small sample: 3 of 20","text":"Now only 3 of 20 patients had a complication. n times p-hat is just 3, below the rule of 10, so the Wald interval is not trustworthy here. The raw Wald formula gives a lower limit below 0 (about -0.006), which is impossible, so statsmodels cuts it off at 0.","code":"x, n = 3, 20\nprint(f'p-hat = {x / n:.2f}')\nfor method in ('normal', 'wilson'):\n    low, high = proportion_confint(x, n, alpha=0.05, method=method)\n    print(f'{method:7s}: ({low:.3f}, {high:.3f})')","out":"p-hat = 0.15\nnormal : (0.000, 0.306)\nwilson : (0.052, 0.360)","figs":[]},{"title":"No events at all: 0 of 20","text":"With zero events, Wald collapses to (0, 0), which claims we are certain the rate is zero after only 20 patients. Wilson gives a sensible upper limit.","code":"for method in ('normal', 'wilson'):\n    low, high = proportion_confint(0, 20, alpha=0.05, method=method)\n    print(f'{method:7s}: ({low:.3f}, {high:.3f})')","out":"normal : (0.000, 0.000)\nwilson : (0.000, 0.161)","figs":[]},{"title":"Coverage test","text":"Suppose the true rate is exactly 10% and n = 20. We simulate 20,000 samples, build both intervals each time and count how often they contain 10%. A good 95% interval should be close to 95%.","code":"import numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(3)\ncounts = rng.binomial(20, 0.10, size=20_000)\nfor method in ('normal', 'wilson'):\n    low, high = proportion_confint(counts, 20, alpha=0.05, method=method)\n    covered = (low <= 0.10) & (0.10 <= high)\n    print(f'{method:7s}: covers the true 10% in {covered.mean():.1%} of samples')","out":"normal : covers the true 10% in 87.5% of samples\nwilson : covers the true 10% in 95.7% of samples","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"x <- 3; n <- 20; phat <- x / n\nme <- 1.96 * sqrt(phat * (1 - phat) / n)\nc(phat - me, phat + me)\nprop.test(x, n, correct = FALSE)$conf.int\nbinom.test(x, n)$conf.int\nprop.test(476, 850, correct = FALSE)$conf.int","expect":"Wald by hand (-0.006, 0.306), the same upper limit as Python; prop.test Wilson (0.0524, 0.3604); binom.test exact (0.0321, 0.3789); poll 476/850 Wilson (0.5264, 0.5930)","note":"prop.test with correct = FALSE gives the Wilson score interval (the default correct = TRUE adds a continuity correction and differs slightly). binom.test gives the exact interval. R has no built-in Wald interval, so the first lines compute it."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (SPSS menus offer exact and Jeffreys intervals under Analyze > Nonparametric Tests > One Sample, but not Wald or Wilson)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / x n.\nBEGIN DATA\n3 20\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE phat = x / n.\nCOMPUTE z = IDF.NORMAL(0.975, 0, 1).\nCOMPUTE wlo = phat - z * SQRT(phat * (1 - phat) / n).\nCOMPUTE whi = phat + z * SQRT(phat * (1 - phat) / n).\nCOMPUTE ctr = (phat + z**2 / (2 * n)) / (1 + z**2 / n).\nCOMPUTE half = z * SQRT(phat * (1 - phat) / n + z**2 / (4 * n**2)) / (1 + z**2 / n).\nCOMPUTE slo = ctr - half.\nCOMPUTE shi = ctr + half.\nLIST VARIABLES=wlo whi slo shi.","note":"Both intervals are written out as formulas, so they match Python: Wald (-0.006, 0.306; Python shows 0.000 because it cuts at 0) and Wilson (0.052, 0.360). For the poll, change the data line to 476 850."},"stata":{"code":"cii proportions 20 3, wald\ncii proportions 20 3, wilson\ncii proportions 20 3, exact\ncii proportions 850 476, wald\ncii proportions 850 476, wilson","note":"cii proportions takes the sample size first, then the number of successes. Wald may be shown as it is (even below 0 for 3 of 20), and Wilson matches Python."},"try":["Change 3 of 20 to 6 of 40 (the same p-hat) and predict how the gap between Wald and Wilson changes.","Try 1 of 20 and look at the Wald lower limit.","In the coverage test, change the true rate to 0.5 and see whether Wald does better."]});
